  {
    "episode_id": 92440,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92440.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Listen, that is amazing. The Navy just celebrated their 242nd birthday. And now 75 years from Mayport, that's pretty significant. And I know during World War II, the significance of Mayport was very important to the war effort. And it's still important to the war effort, and it's important to the southeast economy, obviously. And we miss our big flat top. Hopefully one day down the road, once the Navy has been strengthened back up, as we move toward that 355 ship goal for the Navy, the Blue Water Navy, then hopefully then we'll be able to start looking at possibly a nuclear carrier based here in Jacksonville. But that's down the road. In the meantime, what we have to do, what I think is important, is we need to be able to stabilize our national defense industry here in northeast Florida and southeast Georgia. And to do that, what we need to look at is we have the LCS program, the Freedom Halls are coming in here. And then we have an ARG, the amphibious ready assault group. But what we really need is probably two of those assault groups because when one is deployed, the workers here, they simply have no work. And so those skills get bought, you know, get siphoned off to places like Norfolk and San Diego. Well, they get that skill worker back here when that ARG returns is near impossible. And so if we had an additional ARG when ones deployed, one can be back getting rework and modernized, and that will help us stabilize the workforce here in Jacksonville. And Mayport plays a critical role in being able to do",
